---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const reviewers = [
  "Gilfoyle",
  "Solid Snake",
  "GLaDOS",
  "Master Chief",
  "Fletcher",
  "Delamain",
];
---

<Layout title="Cacophony — Dylan McCurry">
  <span class="eyebrow">Project</span>
  <h1>Cacophony</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    A GitHub Action that runs adversarial pull request reviews from prompt-defined agent lenses —
    because one AI reviewing another AI's code needs more than a single polite opinion.
  </p>
  <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <span class="tag">GitHub Actions</span>
    <span class="tag">Azure AI Foundry</span>
    <span class="tag">No runtime deps</span>
  </div>
  <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/jdylanmc/cacophony" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View on GitHub ↗</a>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    One action invocation runs one Markdown prompt, inspects the checked-out pull request with
    bounded read-only tools, and writes a canonical JSON report, a human-readable Markdown report,
    and action outputs for verdict, severity, report paths, and agent ID. The consuming repository
    owns orchestration — run multiple prompts as sequential steps, matrix jobs, or separate
    workflows. Cacophony itself has no runtime package dependencies, install step, build step, or
    generated distribution bundle.
  </p>

  <h2>Dogfooding: six adversarial reviewers</h2>
  <p>
    The repository runs its own medicine through six canonical adversarial reviewer personas, each
    a distinct prompt under <code>.cacophony/agents/</code>, each running independently on pull
    requests:
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    {reviewers.map((name) => (
      <div class="card" style="cursor: default;">
        <h3>{name}</h3>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Trust boundary for fork review</h2>
  <p>
    Pull-request personas are thin <code>pull_request_target</code> callers of a reusable worker
    that owns the pinned Cacophony invocation, authorization, trusted merge-ref checkout, secret
    scope, and artifact upload. Before checkout, it rejects any event that isn't a pull request,
    allows same-repository pull requests, and allows a fork only when GitHub's
    <code>author_association</code> is <code>OWNER</code>, <code>MEMBER</code>, or
    <code>COLLABORATOR</code> — inspecting pull-request content without executing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create the repository secret <code>CACOPHONY_AZURE_API_KEY</code>.</li>
    <li>Create the repository variable <code>CACOPHONY_AZURE_ENDPOINT</code> with an Azure AI Foundry project or OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</li>
    <li>Pin a release: <code>jdylanmc/cacophony@v0.1.0</code>, or float on <code>@v0</code> / <code>@v0.1</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/projects">&larr; Back to projects</a>
</Layout>
